  "full_response": "Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are specialized kinds of artificial neural networks designed to process and analyze visual data, effectively learning hierarchical features and patterns. Here is a step-by-step breakdown of how CNNs process image data:\n\n1. **Input Layer:**\n   - The input layer takes the image as a multi-dimensional array. For instance, a color image is typically represented as a 3D array with dimensions corresponding to height, width, and color channels (e.g., RGB channels).\n\n2. **Convolutional Layers:**\n   - **Convolution Operation:** The core component of a CNN is the convolutional layer, which applies a set of filters (also called kernels) to the input image. Each filter slides across the image (a process known as \"convolution\") to produce feature maps.\n   - **Feature Extraction:** Each filter detects specific features such as edges, textures, or patterns. By applying multiple filters, the network can extract various features from the image.\n   - **Activation Function:** Non-linear activation functions (commonly ReLU—Rectified Linear Unit) are applied to introduce non-linearity into the model, allowing it to learn complex patterns.\n\n3. **Pooling (Subsampling) Layers:**\n   - **Purpose:** Pooling layers reduce the spatial dimensions of the feature maps, making them computationally manageable and less prone to overfitting. This also helps in achieving translational invariance to some extent.\n   - **Types of Pooling:** The most common type is max pooling, where the maximum value in each window (usually 2x2) is retained, effectively downsampling the image.\n\n4. **Fully Connected (Dense) Layers:**\n   - After several convolutional and pooling layers, the high-level reasoning in terms of classifying the image is carried out via fully connected layers. The output of the final pooling layer is flattened into a 1D array and fed into these densely connected layers.\n   - These layers take the high-level features detected by the convolutional layers and use them to make final predictions through a series of dense interconnections.\n\n5. **Output Layer:**\n   - **Classification:** For classification tasks, the output layer typically uses a softmax activation function to output a probability distribution over various classes, indicating the likelihood of the image belonging to each class.\n\n6. **Training:**\n   - **Backpropagation & Optimization:** While the forward pass computes the output, the backward pass adjusts the weights of the network using backpropagation based on the error (",
